
Quick start
-----------

First install cmake, on ubuntu/debian:
$ sudo apt-get install cmake

Now:
$ ./pamadm --a [--xcfun]
$ cd cbuild
$ cmake ..
$ make

There is nothing special about cbuild.
You can do this instead:
$ ./pamadm --a [--xcfun]
$ mkdir /buildpath
$ cd /buildpath
$ cmake /sourcepath
$ make


Configuring DIRAC with setup
----------------------------

When you are new to cmake, first configure DIRAC with the script setup. This
is not necessary but will help you to get used to cmake commands. Observe how
setup calls cmake and with which command. Experts can use this command
directly to do expert things. It is not difficult.


Configuring DIRAC without setup
-------------------------------

Setup does nothing more that setting some environment variables (FC, CC, ...)
and calling cmake with some flags. This means that you can call cmake directly
if you know how, for example:

$ CC=icc FC=ifort cmake -DENABLE_MPI=1 ..

If you don't remember the cmake command, run setup with --show to see it.


How to build utilities
----------------------

$ cd /buildpath
$ ccmake /sourcepath

Now set ENABLE_UTILITIES to ON, [c]onfigure, then [g]enerate and exit, and finally
$ make
Now you have the utilities!


You prefer GUI?
---------------

No problem. You can configure with GUI:
$ cmake-gui ..
You may have to install cmake-gui for it.


Make install
------------

Make install is very useful to make DIRAC available to other users on the same
machine:
$ cmake -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/path /path/to/source
$ make install


Running tests
-------------

You can run the test suite with:
$ make test


Contributing to the DIRAC Dashboard
-----------------------------------

If you want to contribute to the DIRAC Dashboard
http://repo.ctcc.no/CDash/index.php?project=DIRAC
then let me say that this is an excellent idea.

You don't need to install anything extra - it is as easy as typing:
$ make Experimental
The result of "make Experimental" appears automatically
on the Dashboard.

Before you experiment with "make Experimental", it is recommended
to have a look at the cdash example recipes in the maintenance
directory.

Make Experimental takes a long time (2 hours or more). This is because we have
many very slow tests. For experimenting with make experimental
you can cheat and remove many lines in cmake/Tests.cmake (and do not rerun pamadm).
Later you can regenerate the full cmake/Tests.cmake.

Note that you can set the build name with -DBUILDNAME=foo
or with setup --buildname.


Creating DEB, RPM, and other packages
-------------------------------------

Configure and build DIRAC, then:
$ cpack -G DEB
or
$ cpack -G RPM

Always use the help that is available:
$ cpack --help
